# Lattice: From Human Computers to Planetary Intelligence
## Research extension | 10 November 2026
=> ../index.gmi Lattice Reconstruction Archive
=> spheres.gmi Spheres and meme computers
=> references.gmi References and provenance

This research extension compares real historical computation, fictional planetary computers, philosophical interpretations, and speculative models. It is not part of the recovered fictional LR-0001 dialogue and does not establish shared canon.

## From symbols to processes
Mesopotamian accounting tablets and scribal institutions -> numerical tables and abaci -> human computing bureaux -> NASA human computers -> FORTRAN and electronic computing -> distributed AI and multi-agent systems -> speculative planetary/civilisational computation.

Katherine Johnson, Dorothy Vaughan and Mary Jackson exemplify skilled human mathematical work at NASA; the film Hidden Figures dramatizes their history. Vaughan's FORTRAN work connects the human-computer era to programming.

## Fictional case studies
Doctor Who: Logopolis (1981) — collective block-transfer computation helps counter cosmic entropy.
Doctor Who: The Face of Evil (1977) — damaged computer Xoanon is interpreted as a god.
Doctor Who: The Long Game (2005) — human neural interfaces and manipulated information flows.
Doctor Who: Forest of the Dead (2008) — CAL's human consciousness supports a vast Library computer.
Douglas Adams: Deep Thought calculates 42, the Answer without its Question; Earth is designed as a planetary computer to discover the Question.
Philip K. Dick: VALIS (1981) — fictional revelatory intelligence, informed by Dick's reported experiences; the novel, reports, and metaphysical interpretations are separate records.
Other comparisons: HAL 9000, GERTY, Data, The Machine, GAIA, Culture Minds, Skynet, Poe, Dorian, Cosmic AC, RoboCop and Terminator.

## Philosophical and real-world cases
Structuralism and semiotics: Saussure, Levi-Strauss, Lacan, Lotman; symbolic structures shape thought and meaning. 'Structures speak us' is a thematic paraphrase.
Haraway: cyborg boundaries between humans, technology and institutions.
Bateson: ecology of mind, communication, difference and feedback.
Nick Land and CCRU: technocapitalism as a cybernetic, accelerating process. Claims of literal future AI directing the past are speculative, not demonstrated.
Roko's Basilisk: a hypothetical future-AI decision-theoretic thought experiment, not evidence of actual punishment or retrocausality.
Rachel Carson's Silent Spring (1962): scientific observation -> public interpretation -> environmental movements -> policy/practice -> further ecological feedback. This is historically grounded sociotechnical change, not proof of a conscious planetary mind.

## Universality, incompleteness, and caution
Distinguish epistemic incompleteness, formal computational limits and normative uncertainty. Godel's incompleteness theorems apply to specified formal systems; they do not prove that all societies are incomplete in the same mathematical sense.
Computation does not by itself demonstrate intelligence, agency, sentience or consciousness.
Model boundaries are not reality boundaries. Possibility is not evidence.

## St Elsewhere / Lattice relationship
These references can extend the St Elsewhere universe as comparative fictional, symbolic and processual material while retaining their original authorship and independent fictional continuities. Lattice is a research and reconstruction frame, not a claim of shared canon.
No Inform 7 source or runtime was changed.
